		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=690#comment-19338</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[I&#039;m very glad to come across this site.  I had only one encounter with Shamcher, in 1977 I believe, give or take a year.  I was living in Edmonton, teaching mathematics at the University of Alberta.  That fall an unusual Kuwaiti named Ali, about age 50, appeared and enrolled in some graduate psychology courses.  He also presented himself as a Sufi and I had several occasions to meet him in that role, although our interactions were always just social.  Then one day I found a note in my university mail box announcing an important meeting having to do with education, to be held at this Ali&#039;s house.  Out of curiosity I showed up.  There were about 20 others there, a few of whom I knew.  None of us knew what was going on.  Ali was wearing a green robe and some sort of hat and he announced that we were there to meet a special guest, a man from Seattle named Shamcher.  This elderly man came in with a shock of blond hair and wonderful blue eyes and was introduced.  I was seated on a couch with two other guys and he sat down directly across from us, making a comment about the &quot;three wise men.&quot;  I quickly put my hands over my eyes as one of the &quot;wise monkeys&quot; which got a laugh.  Ali droned on for a while about a charter and starting a school and such.  Then he turned the floor over to Shamcher who smiled and asked in a soft voice if anybody had any questions.  Nobody knew what was going on and there was a few seconds of silence.  Finally, I asked &quot;What&#039;s a good question.&quot;  Going beyond my intended question, he suddenly spoke with a deep and vibrant voice: &quot;A good question is one that grabs you right in the gut and doesn&#039;t let go until you have an answer.&quot;  Then he became again an elderly white-blond gentleman seated modestly across the room.  I never met him after that evening, but that answer has stayed with me ever since.]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>I&#8217;m very glad to come across this site.  I had only one encounter with Shamcher, in 1977 I believe, give or take a year.  I was living in Edmonton, teaching mathematics at the University of Alberta.  That fall an unusual Kuwaiti named Ali, about age 50, appeared and enrolled in some graduate psychology courses.  He also presented himself as a Sufi and I had several occasions to meet him in that role, although our interactions were always just social.  Then one day I found a note in my university mail box announcing an important meeting having to do with education, to be held at this Ali&#8217;s house.  Out of curiosity I showed up.  There were about 20 others there, a few of whom I knew.  None of us knew what was going on.  Ali was wearing a green robe and some sort of hat and he announced that we were there to meet a special guest, a man from Seattle named Shamcher.  This elderly man came in with a shock of blond hair and wonderful blue eyes and was introduced.  I was seated on a couch with two other guys and he sat down directly across from us, making a comment about the &#8220;three wise men.&#8221;  I quickly put my hands over my eyes as one of the &#8220;wise monkeys&#8221; which got a laugh.  Ali droned on for a while about a charter and starting a school and such.  Then he turned the floor over to Shamcher who smiled and asked in a soft voice if anybody had any questions.  Nobody knew what was going on and there was a few seconds of silence.  Finally, I asked &#8220;What&#8217;s a good question.&#8221;  Going beyond my intended question, he suddenly spoke with a deep and vibrant voice: &#8220;A good question is one that grabs you right in the gut and doesn&#8217;t let go until you have an answer.&#8221;  Then he became again an elderly white-blond gentleman seated modestly across the room.  I never met him after that evening, but that answer has stayed with me ever since.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
